1
00:00:00,060 --> 00:00:03,210
Is it in professionals best interest to choose courage?

2
00:00:03,210 --> 00:00:07,470
Will that help them be more awesome and advance their jobs,

3
00:00:07,470 --> 00:00:08,884
or is it better to play it safe?

4
00:00:08,884 --> 00:00:10,067
How do you think about that?

5
00:00:10,067 --> 00:00:12,165
So I think there's basically two answers to that question.

6
00:00:12,165 --> 00:00:14,602
I mean, first of all, depends on your goals.

7
00:00:14,602 --> 00:00:17,562
If your goals are basically to just get ahead potentially

8
00:00:17,562 --> 00:00:20,574
as quickly as possible, then frankly, you and I know there

9
00:00:20,574 --> 00:00:24,009
are lots of organizations where the definition of being awesome at

10
00:00:24,009 --> 00:00:26,758
your job is keeping your head down, doing what you're

11
00:00:26,758 --> 00:00:27,022
told,

12
00:00:27,022 --> 00:00:28,380
and just delivering.

13
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:31,000
And in that regard, you can say choosing courage in

14
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:32,781
the short run, not a great idea.

15
00:00:32,781 --> 00:00:35,933
On the other hand, if you say I want to live a life

16
00:00:35,933 --> 00:00:38,959
where I felt I had agency, where I was authentic,

17
00:00:38,959 --> 00:00:41,804
I was true to myself, I lived my values,

18
00:00:41,804 --> 00:00:44,185
then it's the right choice to make.

19
00:00:44,185 --> 00:00:48,354
Another way to think about it is over what time horizon?

20
00:00:48,354 --> 00:00:52,942
So if you're talking about whether choosing courage will necessarily

21
00:00:52,942 --> 00:00:55,886
put me in line first for the next promotion,

22
00:00:55,886 --> 00:00:57,793
well, maybe, maybe not.

23
00:00:57,793 --> 00:01:00,520
But when you start to look at a longer time horizon,

24
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:03,909
like, will I be proud of the legacy I'm creating?

25
00:01:03,909 --> 00:01:07,843
Will others really remember me and wanna stand with me?

26
00:01:07,843 --> 00:01:10,248
Will I have long term regrets or not?

27
00:01:10,248 --> 00:01:12,935
That's when this choice is so critical.

28
00:01:12,935 --> 00:01:14,728
If you look at re -- the regret literature,

29
00:01:14,728 --> 00:01:18,916
for example, it's pretty well established that people by a large

30
00:01:18,916 --> 00:01:22,972
margin tend to regret inactions. Things they think they should

31
00:01:22,972 --> 00:01:24,368
have done and didn't,

32
00:01:24,368 --> 00:01:27,070
then actions they took that didn't go well.

33
00:01:27,070 --> 00:01:29,781
This is true even, you know, in people who suffer pretty

34
00:01:29,781 --> 00:01:30,619
big consequences.

35
00:01:30,619 --> 00:01:33,555
Whistleblowers, for example, almost none of them say

36
00:01:33,555 --> 00:01:34,764
they regret doing it.

37
00:01:34,764 --> 00:01:37,713
So what I would say to listeners is it depends. If you're talking

38
00:01:37,713 --> 00:01:39,960
about how to be most popular, get ahead tomorrow,

39
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:42,815
well, sticking your neck out is not always the best approach.

40
00:01:42,815 --> 00:01:46,148
If you're talking about living where you or I or Aristotle or

41
00:01:46,148 --> 00:01:48,203
anybody else would call the good life,

42
00:01:48,203 --> 00:01:50,407
then I'd say, yeah, you got to choose courage

43
00:01:50,407 --> 00:01:50,908
sometimes.

44
00:01:50,908 --> 00:01:55,005
And I'd imagine with, sort of, any measure of prudent risk

45
00:01:55,005 --> 00:01:58,282
taking and say, I'm gonna take on this big

46
00:01:58,282 --> 00:02:03,123
project or responsibility or duty or opportunity where the outcome

47
00:02:03,123 --> 00:02:04,315
is uncertain.

48
00:02:04,315 --> 00:02:10,689
I think that a level of that is essential for a career to advance.

49
00:02:10,689 --> 00:02:13,780
Otherwise, you don't seem that special.

50
00:02:13,780 --> 00:02:17,191
It's like, okay, you did your job within the realm

51
00:02:17,191 --> 00:02:19,210
of ordinary responsibilities.

52
00:02:19,210 --> 00:02:23,756
You didn't deliver any really cool, sort of, noteworthy improvements.

53
00:02:23,756 --> 00:02:24,221
So --

54
00:02:24,221 --> 00:02:26,529
Yeah. I mean, okay, I would say if we're

55
00:02:26,529 --> 00:02:27,357
really honest,

56
00:02:27,357 --> 00:02:30,939
there are a few paths probably right to eventually standing out.

57
00:02:30,939 --> 00:02:33,679
One, of course, is to be the absolutely

58
00:02:33,679 --> 00:02:35,265
best political player.

59
00:02:35,265 --> 00:02:35,647
Right.

60
00:02:35,647 --> 00:02:38,104
Attach yourself to the most important people and play

61
00:02:38,104 --> 00:02:40,749
their game and, you know, you'll get ahead to some degree.

62
00:02:40,749 --> 00:02:43,825
Now, for those of us who find that approach distasteful in

63
00:02:43,825 --> 00:02:45,930
a variety of ways, I think you're right.

64
00:02:45,930 --> 00:02:49,639
You have to stand out eventually and with some consistency

65
00:02:49,639 --> 00:02:50,550
in other ways.

66
00:02:50,550 --> 00:02:54,716
And that's where there's such a difference between just being

67
00:02:54,716 --> 00:02:57,702
courageous and being competently courageous.

68
00:02:57,702 --> 00:02:59,400
My book is titled "Choosing Courage."

69
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:01,461
In many respects it should have been titled "Choosing

70
00:03:01,461 --> 00:03:02,188
Competent Courage,"

71
00:03:02,188 --> 00:03:06,112
because indeed, the route to success is not just

72
00:03:06,112 --> 00:03:08,449
speaking up or speaking out,

73
00:03:08,449 --> 00:03:13,155
pushing back against every possible thing you could in

74
00:03:13,155 --> 00:03:17,860
offensive language or with terrible emotional violence,

75
00:03:17,860 --> 00:03:22,627
it's about doing those things in ways to your point that help

76
00:03:22,627 --> 00:03:27,210
you stand out positively. Because not just did you

77
00:03:27,210 --> 00:03:29,763
point out a problem, a path forward,

78
00:03:29,763 --> 00:03:32,910
a way to expand a market, a creative idea,

79
00:03:32,910 --> 00:03:37,408
but you did it in a way that those above you could hear that they

80
00:03:37,408 --> 00:03:38,814
weren't offended by.

81
00:03:38,814 --> 00:03:40,926
Because at the end of the day, right,

82
00:03:40,926 --> 00:03:43,624
you can stand out in positive or negative ways.

83
00:03:43,624 --> 00:03:46,006
And what you're referring to is how do you stand out

84
00:03:46,006 --> 00:03:46,800
in positive ways.

85
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:50,670
And that's about skills when you behave courageously.

86
00:03:50,670 --> 00:03:53,670


